8, T9], [T6, T7, T8]] \u2014 both in **Bamboo (T)** suit? Wait, no! T = Bamboo (\u6761), yes. So both are **in the same suit (Bamboo)**, forming a shifted sequence: T6-T7-T8 and T7-T8-T9 \u2192 this is a **Short Straight** (overlap), worth 2 pts, and potentially **Pure Shifted Chows**?\n\nBut I have two identical chows? No: [T6,T7,T8] and [T7,T8,T9] \u2014 consecutive, overlapping. This qualifies as **Pure Shifted Chows**? Rule says: *three successive chows in the same suit with fixed interval*. Here only two chows. Need three for Pure Shifted Chows (24 pts) or Four Pure Shifted Chows (32 pts). So no.\n\nBut **Short Straight** (1 pt) can apply per pair of consecutive chows. Since I have two chows in same suit with overlapping numbers (T6-T7-T8 & T7-T8-T9), that forms a continuous run T6-T7-T8-T9 \u2192 eligible for **Short Straight** once, worth 1 pt (but listed as 2 pts above \u2014 correction: according to list, Short Straight is 2 pts).\n\nYes: \u201cShort Straight: A hand with two chows in the same suit with successive numbers, like 234 and 567.\u201d Wait \u2014 example is non-overlapping. But rule doesn\u2019t specify must be disjoint. Overlapping (like 678+789) still shows sequential progression. Generally accepted as valid for Short Straight.\n\nThus, I already have **Short Straight** (2 pts) from melds.\n\nNow, hand: W1, T4, B1\u00d72, B5\u00d72, B6, F1.\n\nPossible direction: build **another chow** in Bamboo (e.g., B4-B5-B6 or B5-B6-B7), and pair with B1s or F1.\n\nF1 is East Wind \u2014 matching both seat and prevalent wind. If I form a pung of F1, I get 2+2=4 pts. But only one F1 now, others likely guarded.\n\nAlternatively, try for **Seven Pairs**? I have pairs: B1\u00d72, B5\u00d72. That\u2019s two pairs. Need five total. Possible if I keep drawing duplicates. But slow, and I\u2019m discarding.\n\nBut Seven Pairs requires concealed hand \u2014 I have exposed melds \u2192 invalid! Exposed melds mean cannot qualify for Seven Pairs (must be fully concealed). So ruled out.\n\nSimilarly, **Fully Concealed Hand** (4 pts) is gone due to chi actions.\n\nSo best bet: aim for **All Chows** (2 pts), which allows exposed melds. I already have two chows; need two more and a pair.\n\nCurrent hand: five single tiles and two pairs. If I can form two chows from hand, e.g., using B1-B2-B3 (but B2/B3 scarce), or B4-B5-B6.\n\nB4: 2 left, B5: 3 left, B6: 2 left \u2014 possible.\n\nI have B5\u00d72, B6, so waiting for B4 to complete B4-B5-B6 chow.\n\nAlso have B1\u00d72 \u2014 maybe wait for B2/B3 to form B1-B2-B3.\n\nBut B2: 2 left, B3: 1 left \u2014 possible.\n\nBut competing priorities.\n\nHowever, I must discard one now.\n\nBest discard: remove tiles not contributing to future chows in Bamboo.\n\nOptions:\n- **W1**: Character tile, unrelated to any meld. Only one, no pair, no sequence. Safest discard.\n- **T4**: Bamboo 4, could go into T4-T5-T6, but T5: 2 left, T6: 1 left, and T6 already heavily used. Risky.\n- **F1**: Wind, matches double wind, but unlikely to get two more. Can hold longer.\n- **B1**: Have two, foundation for chow or pair.\n- **B5**: Have two, core of possible B4-B5-B6.\n- **B6**: Part of B5-B6-B7 or B4-B5-B6.\n\nSo **W1** is weakest.\n\nMoreover, playing W1 avoids giving away useful Bamboo tiles.\n\nAlso, W3/W5/W7 all gone \u2014 others are forming sequences in Characters too, so W1 might be safe.\n\nNo one has shown interest in W1 specifically.\n\nReason: Discarding W1 minimizes damage to potential hand development, as it's unpaired, not part of any viable chow, and belongs to a suit not used in my melds. It preserves flexibility in pursuing All Chows with Bamboo and honors.\n\nAnswer: Play W1"}
