- I have terminals: B1, B9, T9 \u2014 so not All Simples.\n- Perhaps **Outside Hand**? Requires every meld to contain terminal or honor.\n  - [T6,T7,T8]: 6,7,8 \u2014 all simples, no terminal \u2014 so not Outside Hand.\n- **One Voided Suit**: I have Bamboo and Dots, no Characters \u2014 so only two suits. Yes! **One Voided Suit** (1 point).\n- **No Honors**: I have no honor tiles in hand or melds \u2014 B5 is not honor, it's Dot-5. So yes, **No Honors** (1 point).\n- **All Chows**: I have two chows and one pung \u2014 not all chows.\n- **Mixed Double Chow**: two identical chows in different suits \u2014 no.\n- **Pure Double Chow**: two identical chows in same suit \u2014 I have [T6,T7,T8] and [T7,T8,T9] \u2014 not identical.\n\nSo minimal fans: One Voided Suit (1), No Honors (1) \u2014 only 2 points.\n\nPlus if I win by discard, and all melds exposed, **Melded Hand** (6 points) \u2014 total 8 points, barely qualifying.\n\nMelded Hand requires:\n- All melds formed by stealing (which they were: Chi T8, Chi T7, Peng B5 \u2014 all from others)\n- Pair must not be stolen; the winning tile must be the 14th tile from another player.\n\nSo if I win by claiming a discard of B1 or B9, I get Melded Hand (6), One Voided Suit (1), No Honors (1) \u2014 total 8 points.\n\nPerfect.\n\nIf I draw the tile myself, I lose Melded Hand, but get Self Drawn (1 point), but then only 1+1+1=3 points \u2014 insufficient.\n\nSo I must win by discard.\n\nTherefore, I need to keep the option open.\n\nNow, which discard is safest and best for this goal.\n\nOptions:\n- Play T9: discard T9. Keep B1\u00d72 and B9\u00d72. Then I am waiting on B1 or B9. Both are possible, and since I have pairs, I can win if someone discards B1 or B9.\n- Play B1: discard one B1. Then I have only one B1, so waiting on B1, B9, or T9. But B9\u00d72 is a pair, so mainly waiting on B1 or T9 to pair.\n- Play B9: similar.\n\nBut if I discard B1, I break the B1 pair, so I need to draw or claim B1 to remake the pair \u2014 less efficient.\n\nSimilarly for B9.\n\nBut if I discard T9, I keep two pairs, so I have two ways to win: B1 or B9.\n\nMoreover, T9 is more likely to be dangerous? But T9 unshown:1, and many players have played T9 \u2014 probably not wanted.\n\nB1 and B9 are more central to my waiting.\n\nAlso, B1 has 2 left, B9 has 3 \u2014 both relatively available.\n\nBy discarding T9, I become a two-way wait on B1 and B9.\n\nThis increases chances of someone discarding one.\n\nWhereas if I discard B1, I am waiting on B1 (to pair), B9 (to win), or T9 (to pair) \u2014 but T9 only one left, and I just discarded it? No, if I discard B1, I keep T9.\n\nBut T9 is only one in hand, so I need another T9.\n\nBut unshown T9:1 \u2014 possible, but less likely.\n\nSimilarly, B1 has 2 left, but if I discard one, I signal I don't need it, but opponents may not know.\n\nIn terms of efficiency, keeping both B1 and B9 pairs maximizes winning opportunities.\n\nTherefore, best to discard T9.\n\nThen I wait for B1 or B9 to be discarded.\n\nAnd aim for Melded Hand (6) + One Voided Suit (1) + No Honors (1) = 8 points.\n\nAnswer.\n\nSituation: I have three exposed melds: two chows in Bamboo ([T6,T7,T8], [T7,T8,T9]) and a pung of B5. My hand has T9, B1\u00d72, B9\u00d72. With prevalent and seat wind being East, but no wind tiles in hand. Unseen tiles show B1 and B9 are relatively available. Valid actions are to discard T9, B1, or B9.\n\nEvaluation: Discarding T9 keeps two terminal pairs (B1 and B9), giving me two ways to win by claiming a discard. This supports achieving \"Melded Hand\" (6 points), as all melds are exposed and formed by stealing. Combined with \"One Voided Suit\" (1 point, only Bamboo and Dots) and \"No Honors\" (1 point), this reaches exactly 8 points. Discarding B1 or B9 would break one pair, reducing efficiency and increasing risk of not completing a legal hand.\n\nReason: To maximize the chance of winning with a minimum 8-point hand, I must preserve both B1 and B9 pairs to enable a two